# Intent: The shared pack loader learns a seventh pack type, phrase (the offline phrases, patch K12), and the flash cache directory moves to format 2 so it can name it. Types are look, language, filler, sound, dance, game, phrase; the size gate of a phrase pack is 150,000 bytes (the sixteen clips are about 60 KB). A format 1 directory (six types, written by the units flashed with TB02) is still read: its checksum covers six entries and the seventh type is empty, so a held look or filler pack survives the update with no data debt; the next directory write is format 2. New hal/maipai_inflate.h: raw deflate to memory with the decompressor's 11 KB state in PSRAM. The ROM's tinfl_decompress_mem_to_mem keeps that state on the caller's stack, and a deflated words.txt inflated on the serial tool task (or any task with a 4 to 8 KB stack) overflowed it, corrupted the heap beside the stack and crashed the unit at the next log line (found on the unit, first window of the packs work); K11, K12 and K13 use the helper. Nothing else in the loader changes: the receiver, the checksum, the zip walker, the ping-pong directory and the power-cut order are the ones the TB02 tests cover.
# Placement: unit (the cache is the robot's own flash)
# Upstream: firmware/main/hal/maipai_pack_core.h and maipai_packcache_core.h (ours, TB02); new hal/maipai_inflate.h (ROM miniz, tinfl_decompress)
# Check after a flash: self.pack.status for type phrase answers held false and fallback true; the packs held before the flash (a look, a filler pack) are still held after it, with cache ok; sending a phrase pack and a language pack (K11, K12, K13), the language pack with a deflated words.txt, answers ok and a reboot with the host off leaves both held.

